# {{.Name}}

An independent Go {{.GoVersion}} project. Its current executable loads and validates
configuration, publishes a typed value, closes its temporary source, reports the
result and exits. It does not start a Worker, Workflow, server or background host.

## Build and test

Run from the project directory:

```sh
GOWORK=off go mod tidy
GOWORK=off go mod tidy -diff
GOWORK=off go test -race ./...
mkdir -p bin
GOWORK=off go build -mod=readonly -o bin/{{.Name}} ./cmd/{{.Name}}
```

Generation performs none of these commands and does not fabricate go.sum.
{{if .Source}}
This is explicit development mode. go.mod contains live replacements into the
selected Framework checkout and its confined SDK directories. Changes there affect
later builds. v0.0.0 is only a local replacement placeholder, not a release.
Do not deploy with developer-specific checkout paths.
{{else}}
The Framework is pinned to {{.Version}}. Its versioned SDK replacement policy is
included automatically because Go does not inherit a dependency's replace rules.
No source tree or external dependency manifest is copied.
{{end}}

## Project responsibilities

- internal/setting: aggregate/application data, defaults and validation.
- internal/bootstrap/options.go: the selected provider's typed inputs and bindings.
- internal/bootstrap/inputs.go: project environment names and captured application overrides.
- internal/bootstrap/boot.go: one shared Boot/Watch path over Framework scenarios.
- cmd/{{.Name}}: process signals, safe result output and exit.

Boot imports Framework configuration, never Adapters or Internal. It supplies an
explicit Provider descriptor and project Schema/Variables. Framework owns source
construction, operation/evidence machinery and cleanup. ReadInputs handles the
explicitly authorized input bindings; document locations carry their layer policy.
Application code extends Settings rather than modifying Framework.

## Explicit provider and format

This project explicitly selects {{.Mode}} / {{.Provider}}. Framework's built-in
package includes both supported provider dependencies, but constructs only the
selected implementation; unused services and credentials are never required. The
DocumentEncoding constant declares {{.Encoding}}; Boot passes that encoding to
strict preparation. The runtime has no --config-source, --config-provider or
--config-format selector. To change provider, explicitly edit the Framework dependency declaration
or generate another profile; changing environment never changes provider.

Runtime inputs are --env and --dotenv, plus
{{if eq .Mode "local"}}--config-dir and --local-config.
Defaults are development and configs.{{else}}required --bootstrap.
The environment default is development.{{end}}
Flags override the matching declared process variables, explicitly selected dotenv,
then these project defaults. Empty is present, not permission to fall back.
Relative paths are relative to the invocation directory.

.env.example is inert. Copy deliberately and select with --dotenv .env. Literal
dotenv accepts KEY=value, single quotes, JSON double quotes and comments; it does
not execute export/interpolation, modify os.Environ or authorize unknown keys.
FATHOMRY_APP_LOCALE is a separate process-only application override.

{{if eq .Mode "local"}}
## Local execution

```sh
./bin/{{.Name}} --env development --config-dir configs
./bin/{{.Name}} --env production --config-dir /mounted/configs
```

Precedence: defaults < required base < required environment < explicit optional
local override < captured application values. A missing production document never
falls back to development. The local example is not automatically loaded; use
--local-config only after deliberately creating the override.
{{else}}
## Remote execution

Copy bootstrap.example.{{.Encoding}} to a protected bootstrap.{{.Encoding}} and
replace its endpoint/namespace/document bindings. Publish the application samples
through your authorized configuration-management process; creation does not publish.

```sh
./bin/{{.Name}} --env production --bootstrap /mounted/bootstrap.{{.Encoding}}
```

Bootstrap contains deployment-selected Nacos acquisition data, not application
Settings. Framework PrepareNacos owns its supported schema, strict loading and
environment-to-key selection; the project supplies the path and selected environment.
Its extension selects YAML/YML, TOML or JSON independently of application encoding.
Namespace and document keys are explicit; environment is not a Namespace ID.
The bootstrap schema grants no write/dynamic-key permissions or implicit keys. An optional
override is another remote key, never a mixed local fallback. Multiple document
reads are not a server transaction.

Only declared FATHOMRY_NACOS_USERNAME, FATHOMRY_NACOS_PASSWORD and
FATHOMRY_NACOS_ROOT_CA_PEM override connection fields. Process values override
dotenv; explicit empty remains empty. Do not commit credentials or pass them in
command history. An unavailable service does not authorize reading local samples.
{{end}}

## Data, evidence and shutdown

Both formats use strict original-document preparation: exact json field names,
per-layer type/unknown-field checks, recursive object overlay and whole-list
replacement. Invalid lower layers cannot be hidden by later ones. Empty TOML is
a valid empty map, not a missing document; empty YAML/JSON rejects. TOML temporal
and nonfinite values are unsupported. Native weak decoding is not substituted.

Boot returns Framework Result with accepted State and released records, including
startup-input and remote-bootstrap reads.
A cleanup error does not erase accepted data. Records are process-local evidence,
not a durable audit log. Main reports acceptance, application name, record count
and the first public error occurrence's code; it never dumps native causes or
configuration/credential payloads. settings.Default is not installed implicitly.

Framework joins its acquired source/operation owners and transfers released facts.
Boot contains no receiver or cleanup engine. Cancellation does not detach cleanup. The first SIGINT/SIGTERM requests cancellation
after restoring default handling; a later signal can force exit, which cannot prove
cleanup. The generated Watch function uses the same project declarations and dependency
selection. Its second return value contains released startup records. The caller
owns the returned Watcher, waits for initial acceptance, reads its Reader, and calls
Close before collecting its final Records. Timed-out Close retains ownership.
Invalid updates retain last-good data; valid updates replace the complete snapshot.
Watch does not update a separate earlier Boot result or start a Worker.
